# Symbolic trigonometric node design · `spec:nrz:cas-trig-design`

**Requirement (Design contract and standing)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-contract`

This design implements the symbolic node family of (`spec:nrz:cas-trig-series-concept`); it advertises no new executable capability. All proposed schemas remain Requirements until implementing commits register their statements, typed premises, semantic versions and independent replay checkers. Formal truncated series, trigonometric integration and physical descriptor implementation are separate work. Registration and tests accompany each capability before it is exposed.

## Closed nodes and graph meaning · `sec:nrz:cas-trig-design-nodes`

**Decision (Three Real operators)** · `dec:nrz:cas-trig-design-operators`

Add `Operator::Sine`, `Operator::Cosine` and `Operator::Arctangent` to the closed enum in `crates/cas/src/model.rs`, beside the existing `NaturalLog`, `Exponential`, `Sigmoid` and `Tanh` family. Each has exactly one operand of `Sort::Value(AtomicSort::Scalar(Field::Real))`, a checked Real ambient and the identical Real result sort and ambient. Extend `validation.rs::operator_sorts` and the ordinary ambient admission path together. A Complex operand refuses even when its value is real; Integer uses the explicit checked `IntegerEmbed`, preserving its original Integer node (`def:nrz:cas-symbol-sorts-integer-graph`). Genuinely Rational-sorted expressions are unsupported: the registry has no Rational-to-Real placement, and a checked Rational embedding requires a separate future design. A rational literal already admitted with Real sort needs no embedding. An equal-spelled `Call` retains only its declared signature, with no trigonometric rule authority. `RealPart` and `ImagPart` construct distinct Real expressions linked to the unchanged Complex original; they never retag it (`def:nrz:cas-symbol-sorts-declarations`).

The operands are plain Real radian coordinates; arctangent returns that same coordinate convention. Checked descriptor lowering is an unresolved dependency, not an existing path to these closed nodes. No physical angle kind enters the CAS sort. Tangent remains a quotient of the closed sine and cosine nodes with an explicit cosine-nonzero premise on the original domain.

**Requirement (Unresolved descriptor lowering interface)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-descriptor-lowering`

Delegate the proposed `CheckedTrigLowering` interface to a bindings-side design: it must consume a trusted built-in function identity, original angle or ratio descriptor and operand, checked conversion evidence and mathematical context, and return the corresponding closed CAS node plus retained source/conversion correspondence and the inverse-result angle descriptor. Trusted built-in selection must be distinct from an equal-spelled declared call. The current bindings checker constructs an opaque Call after conversion, which cannot use these laws; this design does not implement that missing bridge. Cross-crate acceptance evidence belongs in the bindings conformance and integration modules of its existing public test target, including angle-versus-ratio mismatch refusals, exact degree/turn conversion controls and original-operand retention. The CAS test target has no bindings dependency and tests only the mathematical CAS part.

**Requirement (One graph through every operation)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-graph`

Use ordinary `ExpressionDraft` admission, the source construction paths in `source_model.rs`, `source_factory.rs` and `source_build.rs`, and the graph's ordered operand edge at position zero. Construction distinguishes a closed constructor from an opaque declared call without recognizing a spelling as authority. Extend the exhaustive operator dispatches used by source construction, `descriptor.rs`, `substitution.rs` and `substitution_draft.rs`; the new unary nodes introduce no binder. Substitution preserves the exact operator and destination sort, context, ambient and scope, lifts inserted bound references beneath existing binders, and does not recurse into inserted replacements. Traversal visits the child and all placement and context dependencies through the existing graph machinery, never through a parallel expression tree.

Canonical identity in `graph/intern.rs` retains the operator discriminant, ordered child identity, semantic header, sort, ambient placement and scope. Thus sine(u), cosine(u), arctangent(u) and a call named sine remain distinct, while repeated identical mathematical expressions share syntax. Original occurrences, source spans and provenance remain distinct and walkable. Admission and each rule check every original occurrence before rewriting, including undefined operands hidden by zero products, cancellation or a later special-value rewrite (`def:nrz:cas-design-interning`), (`rule:nrz:cas-design-occurrences`).

**Decision (Additive portable inventory)** · `dec:nrz:cas-trig-design-wire`

The executable witness magic is `NRZCAS05`. Extend its closed operator inventory additively: `encoding.rs::operator_tag` and `wire_read.rs` allocate the next three unused tags in the implementing tree, proposed as 58 for Sine, 59 for Cosine and 60 for Arctangent against the inventory through 57. The tags carry no scalar payload; the existing expression grammar carries the single operand and all identity dependencies. The implementing commit checks allocation exhaustively against the then-current inventory, registers encoder and decoder together and pins canonical round trips. No existing tag is reassigned, and `Operator::Series`, `Task::Series` and germ encodings retain their exact meanings and order conventions.

Choose an additive closed-inventory extension under the current magic because existing record layouts and meanings are preserved. This does not promise that an older reader accepts the new tags: unknown tags refuse decoding, with no opaque-call fallback. A changed layout or changed meaning of an existing tag instead requires a replacement magic and an explicit migration decision. Each newly available schema creates a new ordered registry snapshot; requests pin that snapshot and witnesses retain the exact used schema names and versions. The new laws start at semantic version 1, independently of the wire magic; existing law versions remain unchanged. Unsupported schema versions produce Unsupported, never substitution of the newest checker. Inventory validation, premise-shape registration, canonical encoding, dispatch and fresh-store expected-claim replay land atomically (`rule:nrz:cas-design-closed-registration`), (`rule:nrz:cas-design-expected-claim`), (`[CAS-sec:cas:guarantees]`).

The solving design's wire definition, the symbol-sorts wire replacement and the piecewise design still name `NRZCAS04` as current while the code and guide carry `NRZCAS05`; this design allocates against the current magic and edits none of those three sibling documents.

## Checked schema requirements · `sec:nrz:cas-trig-design-schemas`

**Requirement (Closed registration and premise frame)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-register`

Extend the closed `Primitive` dispatcher in `rule.rs`, its `formal_register.rs` inventory and the corresponding checkers. No arbitrary callback or named predicate registers a law. Each schema below records its original operand, full context, exact domain, scope and ordered premises; repeated operands must be identical mathematical graph terms in that frame. The checker reconstructs conclusions and residual conditions rather than accepting supplied endpoints. Premise kinds describe checked shapes; an undischarged context assumption remains a qualified hypothesis, and an unknown obligation cannot become an unconditional theorem (`[CAS-sec:cas:primitive-schemas]`).

Trust classes follow (`def:nrz:cas-solving-design-registry`): F is a primitive with a finite exact checker, T is an explicit mathematical theorem with checked premises, and D is a recipe expanded into admitted applications without a new trusted key. Each proposed schema below carries its class; D recipe keys are not primitive manifest entries (`def:nrz:cas-piecewise-design-registration`). New primitives receive entries in the complete positive weight table as a policy choice of the implementing commit (`dec:nrz:cas-design-search-policy`).

**Requirement (Trigonometric definedness)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-definedness`

Propose T `trig.defined@1`, a closed schema with an operator discriminator restricted to the three new nodes. Its statement is Defined(f(u)) on the exact domain from Defined(u) there, with u and f(u) admitted in the Real ambient. Its sole ordered proof premise is the original operand's `DomainGuard`; admission identities are checked parameters. Replay reconstructs the unary application and checks every original child domain. This is a primitive totality law on defined Real inputs, not a Complex continuation. Extend ordinary definedness dispatch only through this registered law (`[CAS-assum:cas:hyp-domain-guard]`).

**Requirement (Sine chain rule)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-diff-sin`

Propose T primitive `diff.sin@1`: in declared Real coordinate x on domain U, D_x sine(u) = cosine(u) v when D_x u = v. Its ordered premise shapes are `DomainGuard` for original u on U, `Differentiable` for u in x on U, and `Derivative` with original u, coordinate x, output v and domain U. The checker matches every identity, checks the Real placements, reconstructs the ordered product cosine(u) v and retains all premise assumptions and residuals. The conclusion is a derivative judgment, not equality between sine(u) and its derivative (`[CAS-assum:cas:hyp-differentiable]`), (`[CAS-assum:cas:hyp-derivative]`).

**Requirement (Cosine chain rule)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-diff-cos`

Propose T primitive `diff.cos@1`: D_x cosine(u) = −sine(u) v under the same ordered original-definedness, differentiability and child-derivative premises as the sine rule. Replay constructs the negation explicitly, matches the child derivative's coordinate and complete domain, and checks the sine node's definedness through the registered totality law. An output with the minus sign removed fails the reconstructed conclusion even if it happens to coincide at an isolated input.

**Requirement (Arctangent chain rule and denominator)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-diff-atan`

Propose T primitive `diff.atan@1`: D_x arctangent(u) = v/(1+u²). Its ordered premises are original `DomainGuard`, `Differentiable`, child `Derivative`, and `StrictSign` proving 1+u² positive in the same Real domain. Derive this premise through existing `real.sign@1`: its even-power rule proves u² ≥ 0 from original Real definedness, its rational rule proves 1 > 0, and its sum rule proves 1+u² > 0 with the exact same subjects and domain. Replay checks the actual exponent two, exact rational one, ordered sign premises and original definedness before strict positivity supplies the quotient's nonzero guard (`[CAS-thm:cas:primitive-real-sign]`), (`def:nrz:cas-symbol-sorts-real-count`). No premise is inferred merely from the displayed denominator or a numerical sample.

`Cas::prove_strict_sign` currently produces rational signs, declared strict guards and their multiplicative closure; extend that producer to construct the existing even-power and sum certificates, with explicit definedness premises where needed. The sign checker already accepts unsupported partial syntax only with exact definedness evidence for the original subject and reached operands, but its Real dependency closure rejects Complex dependencies even beneath RealPart or ImagPart. It therefore does not yet cover every admitted Real operand. Propose only the minimal extension of the existing `real.sign` schema's subject admission: recognize checked Real component projections as Real subjects while independently checking and retaining their original Complex source and definedness, without ordering the Complex source or retagging it. Keep the existing even-power and sum meanings; register any changed premise or schema meaning under an explicit new version according to (`rule:nrz:cas-design-closed-registration`). Unsupported subject shapes leave the derivative Unresolved until this route is implemented; no parallel trigonometric ordered-field primitive is added.

**Decision (Executable differentiation slice)** · `dec:nrz:cas-trig-design-differentiation`

Extend `calculus.rs` postorder production and independent derivative checking with exactly these three chain rules, sharing memoized expression/context/coordinate requests. The current `scalar_model.rs::Relation` has no definedness, differentiability or sign judgment, and `proof_view.rs::scalar_parts` rejects predicate and solving-sign statements. Existing scalar derivatives do not publish a separate differentiability fact. The following bridge is therefore a required unresolved implementation dependency of executable trigonometric differentiation, not a claim that adding formula cases makes the current path accept these schemas. Operators outside the existing executable slice plus these three additions retain their pending typed derivative requests (`alg:nrz:cas-design-differentiation`).

**Requirement (Reconstructed semantic premise bridge)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-premise-bridge`

Add a version-1 closed semantic-premise view to `proof_model.rs` and `scalar_model.rs`, proposed as `SemanticPremise` and `ScalarPremise` with DomainGuard(subject, domain), Differentiable(subject, coordinate, domain), Derivative(subject, coordinate, output, domain) and StrictSign(subject, sign, domain) variants. Every view retains the complete context, scope, qualified hypothesis closure and guarded residuals of its checked source application. These are reconstructed semantic premise kinds, not extra side-channel proof edges or caller assertions. Extend the scalar proof model's typed premise storage and checker inputs additively; ordinary ordered backward application references remain the only proof dependencies. The new chain-rule schema versions specify each ordered slot's semantic kind, and one checked child derivative may be referenced in both the Differentiable and Derivative slots because the former is its logically implied regularity, not an independently asserted hypothesis.

A DomainGuard view is reconstructed only from checked DefinedDomain, an exact Defined predicate on the same domain, or checked derivative definedness with every original guard retained. A Differentiable view is reconstructed from a checked DerivativeOn judgment for that exact subject, coordinate and domain, whose derivative meaning entails differentiability; this projection inherits all conditions and adds no trusted analytic schema. A Derivative view preserves the full child derivative judgment. A StrictSign view is reconstructed from a checked `real.sign` conclusion or its checked strict predicate with the identical subject and region; NonNegative alone cannot satisfy it. Unsupported source kinds refuse conversion. Existing equality-only conversion remains strict and cannot reinterpret these views as equality endpoints.

`calculus.rs::Deriver` must construct a plan containing the reachable scoped proof applications for original definedness, child derivatives and the arctangent denominator sign derivation, in child-before-parent order. It selects the required ordered source references and requests the registered semantic projections; it cannot fill a slot from an unverified cache or a bare predicate. `scoped_check.rs` must dispatch new chain-rule premises through this view rather than converting every source with `scalar_application`; `proof_view.rs` must independently reconstruct each view from the checked statement and schema slot and reject context, domain, coordinate or subject mismatches. Extend `derivative_check.rs` beyond its existing one-derivative-per-operand contract only for the new registered schemas. Preserve and union all qualified assumptions and residuals, including original divisor exclusions; narrowing a domain requires explicit checked restriction rather than discarding conditions during conversion.

Fresh replay decodes the same ordinary application dependency closure, verifies each source statement, reconstructs the version-1 semantic views and checks the derivative against the caller's exact expected judgment. No serialized premise-kind assertion supplies authority. The premise-shape inventory and its versioned scalar/scoped conversion contract are registered with the new chain rules; existing relation and statement discriminants and schema versions retain their meanings. New view kinds or changed meanings require explicit versioning and unknown versions refuse. If production, conversion, sign derivation or fresh replay lacks any required view, the dependent whole derivative stays Unresolved; a supplied missing or changed premise is invalid evidence. Output simplification follows checked derivative construction with all conditions retained.

**Requirement (Nested derivative and premise refusal twins)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-nested-derivative`

On the full declared Real x domain with no extra assumptions, check D_x x=1; its checked derivative supplies the Differentiable view for x, and original domain checking supplies DomainGuard(x). Applying `diff.sin@1` yields D_x sine(x)=cosine(x)·1. That checked inner derivative supplies both the outer child Derivative and the Differentiable(sine(x), x) view, while `trig.defined@1` supplies the inner function's DomainGuard. The outer application yields D_x sine(sine(x))=cosine(sine(x))·(cosine(x)·1), followed only by checked unit simplification. The required fixture replays this discharged result in a fresh store with empty hypothesis and residual sets. Its refusal twin removes the outer regularity source reference or replaces it by a derivative of another operand or coordinate; the checker must refuse despite a plausible final formula. A producer lacking the bridge reports Unresolved rather than claiming this fixture passes.

**Requirement (Integer half-Pi recognition prerequisite)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-half-pi`

Closed constant preparation already checks original finite-real domains and exact normalization equality (`[CAS-def:cas:constant-preparation]`), (`[CAS-thm:cas:constant-closed-preparation]`). No existing statement identified in the primitive guide, constants and rules, solving or piecewise designs supplies recognition of k·Pi/2 with a retained integer witness. Add the named F prerequisite primitive `trig.half-pi-witness@1`: its checked conclusion records original Real u, an exact integer k and u = k·Pi/2 in the identical context and domain. Version 1 admits a concrete arbitrary-precision signed integer witness, not an uninstantiated symbolic integer parameter.

This primitive introduces a new conclusion form with its own finite exact checker; it is class F, not a derived recipe or a new transcendental axiom. Ordered premises are original `DomainGuard` and `Equality` proving u equals the graph product of the exact named Pi and the rational k/2. The integer witness is retained as canonical signed exact data; the checker verifies integrality, reconstructs k/2 by exact rational arithmetic and independently replays the equality, including original divisor guards. A normalized rational coefficient q qualifies only after exact arithmetic establishes 2q = k with denominator one. Zero is accepted with k=0 by checked equality even if normalization erased its Pi factor. The record retains original u and k, and replay cannot replace either. No comparison of printed digits, approximate Pi, numerical proximity or producer cache supplies recognition. Unproved recognition remains unresolved; malformed or forged evidence refuses.

**Requirement (Half-Pi statement and certificate payload)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-half-pi-payload`

Propose `Statement::HalfPiWitness` in `proof_model.rs`, with ordered fields original operand u, integer k, context and domain. Carry k using the existing canonical integer-valued Rational encoding: denominator exactly one, canonical sign and reduced magnitude, canonical zero and bounded component bits, with no machine-integer narrowing. Allocate statement subtype 31 against current subtypes 0–30 and certificate mode 19, proposed `Certificate::HalfPiWitness`, against modes 0–18; recheck next-free allocation at implementation. These are independent namespaces from outer statement record tag 10 and operator tags 58–60. The version-1 certificate repeats the ordered u, k, context and domain fields for exact comparison with the conclusion, followed by the two ordered premise slot references: slot 0 original DomainGuard(u, domain), slot 1 Equality(u, k·Pi/2, domain). Both slots identify earlier applications in the ordinary premise list; no embedded acceptance bit is admitted. The enclosing judgment context must equal the payload context, and all source identities, domains and scopes must agree.

Extend `statement.rs` encoding, identity and dependency traversal and `wire_statement.rs` decoding with the new closed forms. Traverse u, context and domain as typed graph dependencies and both proof premises through ordinary application dependencies; k is bounded value payload, not an unlinked graph node. The equality dependency retains the exact Pi expression and every original guard. The checker reconstructs k/2 from k, independently replays that equality and domain evidence, and validates the complete expected statement. Malformed or noncanonical integer bytes refuse before publication. Exact normalization supplies only a candidate coefficient; the independently replayable equality supplies authority. `PreparedConstant` has no portable proof or context-discharge authority. The symbolic witness has no approximate-Pi or numerical-enclosure dependency.

**Requirement (Exact special values)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-values`

Propose T primitive schemas `trig.sin-half-pi@1` and `trig.cos-half-pi@1`, each taking the checked half-Pi witness as its ordered premise. For its retained integer k, sine(k·Pi/2) is respectively 0, 1, 0, −1 and cosine(k·Pi/2) is 1, 0, −1, 0 for residues 0, 1, 2, 3. Each checker recomputes Euclidean k=4q+r with 0≤r<4 using exact integer arithmetic, including negative k, and reconstructs the exact rational output for the original operand. The witness carries the operand's definedness and equality premises; no normalization can discard them.

Propose T primitives `trig.atan-zero@1` and `trig.atan-one@1`, concluding arctangent(u)=0 and arctangent(u)=Pi/4 from ordered original DomainGuard and Equality premises identifying u with exactly 0 or 1. Replay checks the constant, original subject and exact named Pi, without evaluating a transcendental function. The named D recipe `trig.atan-minus-one@1` retains original definedness and equality u=−1: checked equality congruence first identifies arctangent(u) with arctangent(−1), parity applied at one gives arctangent(−1)=−arctangent(1), `trig.atan-one@1` gives arctangent(1)=Pi/4, and checked negation congruence and equality transport yield arctangent(u)=−Pi/4. Replay checks that complete expansion; the recipe key is not a primitive manifest entry. Other rational inputs acquire no rational-Pi conclusion.

**Requirement (Parity identities)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-parity`

Propose T primitives `trig.sin-odd@1`, `trig.cos-even@1` and `trig.atan-odd@1`. On defined Real u they conclude sine(−u)=−sine(u), cosine(−u)=cosine(u) and arctangent(−u)=−arctangent(u), respectively. Each takes the original DomainGuard for u; checked negation and trigonometric totality supply the composed domains. The checker reconstructs both sides with exactly the same u, context and scope. Matching printed names, different declarations or different operands cannot satisfy the repeated metavariable.

**Requirement (Pythagorean identity)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-pythagorean`

Propose T primitive `trig.pythagorean@1`: sine(u)²+cosine(u)²=1 on the exact domain of defined Real u. Its ordered premise is the original DomainGuard; the checker requires the same u in both closed nodes, exact exponent two, unit coefficients and exact rational one. It replays both composed domains before establishing EqualOnDomain. This is a registered analytic identity, not a polynomial proof treating sine and cosine as independent indeterminates.

**Requirement (Principal arctangent range)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-range`

Propose T primitive `trig.atan-range@1`: for defined Real u, −Pi/2 < arctangent(u) < Pi/2. The sole ordered premise is the original DomainGuard. The conclusion is the conjunction of strict positivity of arctangent(u)+Pi/2 and Pi/2−arctangent(u), retaining exact named Pi and rational half in the same context and domain. Replay reconstructs both strict comparisons and checks their common original node. Both endpoints are excluded; replacing either strict inequality by endpoint membership cannot establish this schema. The law selects the principal real inverse and grants no Complex branch or numerical enclosure.

## Phase consumers and exclusions · `sec:nrz:cas-trig-design-phase`

**Decision (Phase charts without a new primitive)** · `dec:nrz:cas-trig-design-phase-charts`

Retain the mathematical principal argument contract −Pi<theta≤Pi and partition of (`ex:nrz:decision-phase-chart`), (`ex:nrz:decision-phase-boundaries`). Mathematically, purely imaginary nonzero impedances have phase +Pi/2 or −Pi/2 according to the imaginary sign, and a negative real part with a proved exact singleton zero imaginary part owns Pi; an enclosure merely containing zero does not. These are mathematical requirements, not reachable checked phase conclusions in the current registry. Even z=i lacks a registered judgment connecting positive-imaginary-axis membership to argument Pi/2. Signs and trigonometric special values alone do not supply that connection.

Name the missing prerequisite `CheckedPhaseArgument`, a future proposal for a checked phase/argument statement family retaining the original Complex target, Real component correspondence, complete chart cover and disjointness, exact axis laws, quadrant agreement, principal branch ownership and original-target replay. Its schemas and checkers are unresolved dependent work, as are phase consumer integration fixtures. A general two-argument node remains an open implementation choice and is not delivered here; a descriptor-side recipe would still need this proof authority. RealPart and ImagPart create coordinates without retagging the Complex target. Preserve the angle-difference role, radian convention and wrapped subtraction of (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-kinds`), and the phasor-domain and outcome requirements of (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-phasor-domains`). These cited contracts supply mathematical content only; their correctness supplies no checker.

The ratio b/a divides by zero on the purely imaginary axes and arctangent(b/a) loses the quadrant for negative a. The origin has undefined phase; a region straddling a cut or zero without a certified unique chart stays undecided. A centre estimate cannot choose a branch, and an exact owned cut point proves no neighborhood across that cut. This design's CAS fixtures stop at mathematical coordinate, sign and trigonometric facts; they cannot publish a phase judgment or claim descriptor lowering is implemented.

**Caveat (Certified enclosures excluded)** · `cav:nrz:cas-trig-design-enclosures`

Certified sine, cosine and arctangent enclosures remain excluded under (`cav:nrz:cas-trig-series-enclosures-later`).

## Verification and resource evidence · `sec:nrz:cas-trig-design-verification`

**Requirement (Four existing test locations)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-test-layers`

Adopt the four location layers of (`dec:nrz:cas-solving-design-test-layout`) and (`[CAS-sec:cas:testing]`): inline module-private unit tests, crate tests in `src/tests/`, public conformance modules and public integration modules. Place each assertion in the outermost layer that can express it. Public modules live in `tests/conformance/trig.rs` and `tests/integration/trig.rs` beneath the single `tests/public.rs` target, never new top-level test targets. Shared setup lives in `src/testing/` with the existing public/private entrypoint separation; inline unit tests build their own setup and never import that harness. Preserve location-derived test inventory labels.

**Table (Evidence by test location)** · `tab:nrz:cas-trig-design-test-locations`

|Location layer|Admission and mathematics|Replay, whole requests and resources|
|--------------|-------------------------|------------------------------------|
|Inline unit tests in the owning module|Private operator-tag dispatch, integer residue arithmetic and checker details only when module-private access is necessary; independently transcribed expected values.|Private malformed-record and charged-boundary controls with local setup; no shared harness imports.|
|Crate tests in `src/tests/trig.rs`|Crate-internal interning keys, original occurrences, capture-safe substitution under nested binders, ambient dependencies and traversal; distinct operators and opaque calls.|Internal semantic-premise joins and scalar/scoped conversions, rollback snapshots and publication boundaries; shared dependency-checker controls preserve root-count and RealExact consumers' independence and sort restrictions.|
|Public conformance in `tests/conformance/trig.rs`|Unary Real admission; Complex, invalid embedding and scope refusals; accepted zero after its Pi factor disappears, all four signed residue classes and k beyond machine-integer size; no-special-value outcomes for Pi/3 and a rational approximation of half Pi; arctangent at 0 and ±1; premise-checked chain rules, parity, Pythagorean identity and strict range endpoints.|Accepted-control mutations of context, coordinate, operand, integer witness, assumptions, tags and schema versions; accepted 1+RealPart(z)²>0 on a proved domain paired with changed-source, missing-definedness and direct-Complex-subject refusals; old series/germ canonical fixtures; deterministic refusal and literal N/N−1 boundaries.|
|Public integration in `tests/integration/trig.rs`|Construct original graphs, replay the discharged sine(sine(x)) derivative with its regularity projection, and retain hidden undefined factors and tangent pole premises; consumer tests cover only mathematical CAS facts, with phase proof and descriptor lowering fixtures explicitly unresolved.|Export complete proof closures and replay in fresh stores against independently built expected claims; cold production, warm authenticated reuse and cumulative lifecycle budgets.|

The special-value oracle uses independently written exact integer residue arithmetic and a hand-transcribed value table, never the producer's recognizer or simplifier. Derivative and identity oracles reconstruct typed premises and expected graph terms separately from production. Numerical samples cannot prove laws or range endpoints. Replay controls preserve an accepted unchanged witness, mutate one field, and require refusal or a different valid judgment that fails expected-claim comparison. No test oracle consults the producer's success cache or shares its theorem-producing path. Interrupted or exhausted requests publish no partial theorem; unavailable evidence, invalid evidence, unsupported operations, undefined phase and undecided selection remain distinct (`def:nrz:engineering-refusal-meaning`).

**Table (Trigonometric stopping outcomes)** · `tab:nrz:cas-trig-design-outcomes`

|Outcome|Trigonometric boundary|
|-------|----------------------|
|Unresolved|A valid derivative request lacks a child derivative or denominator sign proof; half-Pi recognition lacks an exact equality or an integer witness.|
|Unsupported|A witness requests an unavailable schema version, or a request asks for an undelivered trigonometric enclosure or two-argument primitive.|
|Invalid|A unary node has a Complex operand, wrong arity or scope; a forged integer witness, premise join or known-tag replay record fails checking.|
|Refused|A valid request cannot meet its demanded unconditional or whole-domain contract, such as tangent on a domain with a proved cosine zero; preserve the underlying domain reason.|
|Exhausted|The caller's Work, integer-bit, depth, staged, retained, output or witness allowance is insufficient; retain charged work and publish no partial claim.|

Output-envelope exhaustion after a theorem has already been checked preserves the existing optional `NotExported` response; it does not retrospectively mean failure to establish that theorem. These rows describe outcome categories without inventing interchangeable API variants; a low-level `StrictSignUnavailable` must not be promoted to a negative mathematical claim, and an undecided phase chart remains undecided.

**Table (Required mutation controls)** · `tab:nrz:cas-trig-design-mutations`

|Mutation from an accepted control|Required independent rejection|
|---------------------------------|------------------------------|
|Drop the cosine derivative minus sign|Reconstructed derivative output differs for a nonzero child derivative and sine factor.|
|Change the Pythagorean cosine coefficient to 2|The exact unit coefficient and reconstructed equality fail.|
|Apply parity across different operands|Repeated operand identity and declaration frame fail.|
|Include either arctangent range endpoint|Strict range conclusion and expected-claim matching fail.|
|Admit a Complex operand, including a real-valued Complex expression|Real sort and ambient admission fail before rule checking.|
|Treat an equal-spelled opaque call as a closed node|Operator discrimination refuses the law while the opaque syntax remains admitted.|
|Tamper with a wire tag or replay premise record|Unknown tags refuse; changed known tags, joins or claims fail replay or expected-claim comparison.|
|Change k or omit integrality in the half-Pi witness|Exact equality, integer admission or independently recomputed residue fails.|
|Remove the arctangent denominator positivity premise|Typed ordered premises and quotient definedness fail.|
|Erase an original undefined child during rewriting|Original occurrence domain checking refuses before publication.|
|Remove or change a nested derivative semantic premise|The checked view rejects a missing regularity source or changed operand, coordinate, domain, context or residual hypotheses.|
|Malformed or noncanonical integer payload, including a nonunit denominator|Half-Pi decoding or integrality checking refuses; arbitrary-size canonical integers remain accepted within budget.|
|Drop an original divisor guard from a half-Pi equality|Replay refuses even if normalization yields an integer multiple or zero.|
|Claim a special value for Pi/3, a rational approximation of half Pi or an opaque Pi impostor|Valid unrecognized inputs return no special value; a forged half-Pi witness is rejected as invalid evidence.|
|Change the Complex source or remove definedness from accepted 1+RealPart(z)²>0|Exact projection correspondence and original domain checks refuse; a direct Complex subject also refuses.|
|Erase z≠0 from RealPart(1/z)|The Real result retains the original reciprocal's nonzero guard; the sign proof cannot discard it.|
|Broaden shared dependency checking for root counts or RealExact presentation|Regression controls retain their independent source checks and existing sort restrictions; projection support grants no blanket Complex admission.|

**Requirement (Measured boundaries without invented figures)** · `req:nrz:cas-trig-design-test-work`

Implementing commits measure complete operations separately: node/source admission, interning hit and miss, substitution, traversal, wire encoding/decoding, derivative generation, half-Pi recognition and residue arithmetic, independent schema checking, retained lookup, fresh expected-claim replay and final publication. Pin each successful Work cost N and refusal at N−1 in isolated fixtures, including prior cumulative usage; pin component/intermediate integer bits, expression depth, staged and retained payload, witness and output bytes separately where consumed. Charge failed work, bound intermediate arithmetic before allocation, and compare full graph snapshots after refusal. Cold and warm costs are distinct measurements. Follow (`req:nrz:enclosures-design-test-work`) and (`def:nrz:engineering-budget-boundary`): a first failed charge is not a completed cost measurement. This design invents no figures or policy ceiling increases.

## Semantic dependencies and evidence boundaries · `sec:nrz:cas-trig-design-landing`

The dependency order is mathematical: closed graph admission and original-domain evidence precede use of the new nodes in checked laws; the semantic premise bridge and sign proof route precede executable chain rules; integer half-Pi recognition precedes half-Pi special values. Parity and the value at one precede the derived minus-one recipe. Identities and range need admitted nodes and definedness but do not inherently require a derivative producer. Phase arguments and descriptor lowering remain separate unresolved dependencies. Each exposed capability must have its statement meaning, typed premises, registration, bounded producer where supplied, independent checker, codecs, fresh expected-claim replay and resource/refusal evidence together; representation alone advertises no executable service.

**Step (Nodes, sorts and portable graph)** · `step:nrz:cas-trig-design-node-lane`

The graph capability comprises Real admission, original occurrences, interning, source construction, capture-safe substitution, traversal and additive wire tags; totality requires its registered checked law. Relevant extension points beneath `crates/cas/src/` are `model.rs`, `validation.rs`, `descriptor.rs`, `graph/intern.rs`, `source_model.rs`, `source_factory.rs`, `source_build.rs`, `substitution.rs`, `substitution_draft.rs`, `encoding.rs`, `wire.rs`, `wire_read.rs`, `rule.rs`, `rule_check.rs`, `formal_register.rs`, `proof_model.rs`, `statement.rs`, `wire_statement.rs` and `lib.rs`, with proposed `trig.rs` for closed law checking. Canonical bytes, typed rejection controls and graph lifecycle evidence are part of the capability boundary.

**Step (Derivative schemas and executable slice)** · `step:nrz:cas-trig-design-derivative-lane`

Executable differentiation depends on the graph capability, reconstructed semantic-premise views, regularity projection from checked child derivatives and the existing sign rules with their narrowly checked projection extension. Formula production, scalar/scoped conversion, domain and hypothesis preservation and independent fresh replay form one capability boundary; the nested derivative and refusal twin are required evidence. Relevant extension points beneath `crates/cas/src/` are `calculus.rs`, `scalar_model.rs`, `proof_model.rs`, `proof_check.rs`, `derivative_check.rs`, `scoped_check.rs`, `proof_view.rs`, `statement.rs`, `wire_statement.rs`, `rule.rs`, `rule_check.rs`, `formal_register.rs`, `encoding.rs`, `wire_read.rs`, `trig.rs`, `strict_sign.rs`, `strict_sign/build.rs`, `solving/real/closure.rs` and `solving/elementary/total.rs`. Changes to shared dependency inspection preserve root-count and RealExact restrictions independently of the new sign capability.

**Step (Integer witness, identities and range)** · `step:nrz:cas-trig-design-identity-lane`

The half-Pi capability includes the new statement, certificate, canonical integer payload, independent equality/domain premises and dependency traversal before special-value laws consume it. Parity, special values, Pythagorean identity and strict principal range each require their own registered checker and mutation controls; the minus-one recipe remains an expanded derivation. Relevant extension points beneath `crates/cas/src/` are `trig.rs`, `proof_model.rs`, `statement.rs`, `wire_statement.rs`, `rule.rs`, `rule_check.rs`, `formal_register.rs`, `encoding.rs`, `wire_read.rs` and `lib.rs`. Consumer evidence here covers exact coordinates, sign statements and symbolic identities only, never an unavailable phase or descriptor judgment.

Across these boundaries, evidence uses inline private tests, `crates/cas/src/tests/trig.rs`, the conformance and integration `trig.rs` modules beneath `crates/cas/tests/`, their existing module entrypoints and shared `crates/cas/src/testing/` setup. The CAS guide template `templates/crates/cas/README.md.j2` and render describe only registered capabilities; this design's template, render and `data/documents.toml` registration remain a matching documentation unit. Complete workspace gates, documentation fixed point, independent oracles and measured N/N−1 boundaries establish each implemented capability's evidence without prescribing commit counts, file ownership or delivery operations.

